---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const compCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Most similar systems design', note: 'Compare countries ALIKE in everything except the outcome — the shared factors are controlled, the differing one is the suspect. Mill\'s method of difference, at country scale.' },
    { math: '2 · Most different systems design', note: 'Countries UNALIKE in everything except the outcome — whatever survives the variation is the explanation. Finds necessary factors where the similar design finds sufficient ones.' },
    { math: '3 · Case studies are not lesser science', note: 'Deep knowledge of one case generates hypotheses, tests mechanisms, and catches what large-N regressions blur. Process tracing: following the causal chain inside one case like a detective (Bennett & Checkel).' },
    { math: '4 · The n = 200 problem', note: 'Only ~200 countries exist — and they aren\'t independent (diffusion, shared history, global shocks). Comparative politics lives with small n and interdependence: careful designs, convergent evidence, and humility about causal certainty.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/advanced/comparative-politics-methods">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Comparative politics is <strong>political science's laboratory: countries as cases</strong>.
    Since you can't randomise nations, the field built rigorous observational designs — paired
    comparisons, process tracing, and disciplined case selection — to extract causal answers
    from a world of ~200 non-randomised units.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the two classic designs, the case-study defence, and the small-n reality" widget="equationStep" config={compCfg} />

  <h2>Classic comparative findings</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Why nations democratise:</strong> modernisation theory (development → democracy), elites bargaining (Moore: "no bourgeoisie, no democracy"), and diffusion waves — the competing explanations each explain a subset of cases, which is the finding.</li>
    <li><strong>Why some states fail:</strong> colonial institutional legacies (the economics lesson's Acemoglu-Robinson), geography, ethnic configurations, rents (oil curse). Comparative work sorted which factors travel.</li>
    <li><strong>Revolution and regime change:</strong> Skocpol's comparative analysis (France, Russia, China) found revolutions come from state crises + elite splits + organised peasants — not simply misery. Comparative method at its most influential.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixed methods is the current standard:</strong> quantitatively screen many cases, qualitatively trace a few — the two method families turned out to be complements (the statistics lesson's design logic, applied across borders).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Mill's methods; Skocpol, States and Social Revolutions (1979); Moore, Social Origins (1966); Bennett & Checkel, Process Tracing (2015).</p>
</TopicPage>
